    "lectures": {
      "control": "Classroom reading: Understanding business record keeping. A business maintains records that describe activities in an organized manner. A document is an entry about an event; it supports later review. Business records are materials already created and retained for future reference. They can include forms on paper and entries in electronic systems. Consistently describing each event can make several documents easier to locate during a review. Later readers can therefore understand records prepared in earlier reporting periods. Each business maintains records for activities, which can differ from another business's documents. Differences in these records can reflect departmental responsibilities and interact with local filing practices. Clear organization helps participants distinguish document titles, responsible employees, and previously recorded information. This reading explains documentation; it does not prescribe an ordering rule.",
      "causal_education": "Classroom reading: Understanding supply-chain dynamics. A supply chain links businesses through orders and physical deliveries. An order is a request for goods; it is not an immediate receipt. Outstanding replenishment orders are requests already placed but not yet received. They can include goods in transit and quantities awaiting upstream fulfillment. Repeatedly responding to one shortage can create several requests before any delivery arrives. Later arrivals can therefore reflect decisions made in earlier weeks. Each business observes incoming requests from its customer, which can differ from final customer purchases. Changes in these requests can propagate through successive businesses and interact with delivery delays. Shared information helps participants distinguish current physical stock, customer obligations, and previously requested replenishment. This reading explains relationships; it does not prescribe an ordering rule."
    },
